2M. MIDI EVENT BUFFERS - one time-ordered event buffer per MIDI port:
    plain MIDI 1.0 messages + frame. Before 2G: graph MIDI ports are
    MIDI_BUFs from the start, and it fixes today's bugs on its own.
    Raw MIDI, not CLAP events, internally: JACK, LV2, CLAP's MIDI dialect,
    sampler and synth all speak it; CLAP note events only at the CLAP
    boundary.
    Today (JACK_MIDI_CONT: type/pitch/vel arrays, scanned per frame):
    - synth keeps only the LAST event per frame - chords lose notes.
    - note-on velocity 0 (= note-off, many keyboards) starts a silent
      synth voice, the note hangs.
    - LV2 writes buf_size bytes from a 3 byte array - sysex reads past it.
    - app_jack_return_notes_vels_rt reads buffer[1..2] whatever the size -
      1 byte messages (clock, active sensing) read garbage.
    - CLAP drops everything but notes (empty branches), even in the MIDI
      dialect; several note ports are appended, not sorted by time.
    - sampler/synth scan every event for every frame: frames x events.
    - every context includes jack_funcs.h just for MIDI.

M1. util_funcs/midi_buf.c/.h, no JACK. MIDI rules on top of
    uniform_buffer - not a second byte arena (UB_EVENT already holds the
    CLAP event lists). One UB_EVENT per port, fixed capacity (e.g. 4 KiB),
    allocated on [main-thread]. An item is {uint32 frame, bytes}, the byte
    count = ub_item_get_size - 4 - sysex fits, no special case. Items are
    not padded (padding would change ub_item_get_size), so frame is
    read/written with memcpy - alignment safe, a plain load on x86/ARM64.
    API: new/free [main-thread]; clear(b, nframes), push(b, frame, data,
    size) -> false when full, iter/next, merge(dst, srcs, n) (stable k-way
    by frame - the graph's MIDI "summing") on rt.
    Rules, only at push: frames never go back (an earlier one is moved to
    the last frame - a note-off is never lost); frame >= nframes or a size
    that does not fit the status byte -> dropped; note-on vel 0 ->
    note-off; drops/overflow counted in an atomic, [main-thread] logs it.
    Same frame keeps push order (retrigger: off then on).
    Decoders static inline in midi_buf.h (type/channel/is_note_on/expected
    size...) - push needs them too, so no separate midi_msg.h; no status
    byte checks by hand anywhere else.
    uniform_buffer fixes first: ub_list_reset memsets the whole arena and
    both index arrays every reset - O(capacity) per port per cycle, CLAP's
    event lists too; resetting items/current_size is enough.
    ub_item_get_size returns -1 as uint32_t on error, its comment says 0.

M2. JACK adapters, the only jack_midi_* users: app_jack_midi_in_rt(port,
    nframes, MIDI_BUF *) and app_jack_midi_out_rt(port, nframes, const
    MIDI_BUF *). JACK_MIDI_CONT, app_jack_init/reset/clean_midi_cont,
    app_jack_return_notes_vels_rt, app_jack_midi_clear_buffer_rt and
    app_jack_midi_events_write_rt go - replaced, no twins. Each context
    owns a MIDI_BUF per MIDI port until 2G moves them into graph ports.

M3. Consumers render in segments - per event: render [pos, ev.frame),
    handle ev, pos = ev.frame; then render [pos, nframes). Events +
    frames instead of frames x events, chords work.
    - sampler: per sample, segments around its note-ons.
    - synth: per osc, segments; vel 0 already a note-off (M1).
    - CLAP clap_input_events_prepare: note ports merged by frame (fixes
      the order); MIDI dialect -> every message raw (CLAP_EVENT_MIDI,
      sysex CLAP_EVENT_MIDI_SYSEX); CLAP dialect -> notes as
      NOTE_ON/NOTE_OFF, the rest raw. Params at frame 0 stay first.
    - LV2: events copied into the evbuf with their real size; MIDI out
      pushed into the out MIDI_BUF, then app_jack_midi_out_rt.
    DONE WHEN: a chord from a sequencer plays every note on the synth; a
    keyboard sending note-on vel 0 releases notes; CC and pitch bend
    reach a CLAP synth in the MIDI dialect; sysex into an LV2 plugin
    passes ASan/valgrind; a sampler retrigger on the same frame works.

2G. INTERNAL GRAPH - the app routes its own ports, JACK only at the edges.
    Exposed to JACK: master_out L/R, master_in L/R, 1-2 MIDI in, 1 MIDI
    out. Every other port is internal, connected by the app.
    Why: latency (2L) - processing order from the connections (no hidden
    period), a delay per connection (dry + wet splits align), a small and
    fast JACK latency callback; no reliance on how pipewire-jack handles
    own -> own connections; routing saved with domain identity; dozens
    fewer JACK/PipeWire ports; the backend becomes swappable.
    Lost: per plugin JACK ports for other programs (G10 brings an opt-in
    back), internal routing invisible to qjackctl/Helvum/jack_lsp.
    ORDER: after 2M; before 2T, 2L (except L1b/L1c - needed either way)
    and 3 (SAVING) - it changes what the routing state is.
    Facts it builds on:
    - contexts reach ports only through app_jack_* with an opaque handle:
      create_port_on_client, get_buffer_rt, unregister_port, port_rename;
      MIDI (after 2M) only as MIDI_BUFs. The graph can sit behind the same
      calls.
    - the UI never sees JACK: routing is root_ops' CONNECT action over the
      DATA_LIST_NS_PORTS lists in app_data (group_key = owner).
    - trk_audio_process_rt calls each context once for all its owners, in
      a fixed order; master is master_in -> memcpy -> master_out.
    - every synth osc has its own MIDI in + outs (the metronome osc only
      outs), so one node per osc is natural.
    - smp_init, plug_init, clap_plug_init and synth_init get trk_jack as
      their backend, and all four contexts include jack_funcs.h.

G1. Per owner process functions, behaviour unchanged. Split the loop
    bodies: clap_process_data_rt / plug_process_data_rt -> one plugin,
    synth_process_rt -> one osc (the metronome osc a source node), the
    sampler one, master one. trk_audio_process_rt calls them in today's
    order. The per context ui_to_rt message reading stays at cycle start.
    Each returns whether it wrote its outputs this cycle (G5).
    Runnable alone - the graph later only changes who calls them.

G2. Graph module (engine/graph.c/.h, knows no backend). [main-thread]
    owns the graph, [audio-thread] only sees a compiled plan (G4).
    Node = owner (tag, uid) + process fn + user_data + latency L.
    Port = heap record that never moves: node, type (audio/midi), flow,
    name, buffer (audio) or MIDI_BUF (midi), key. Keys minted like the
    JACK cache's - stable for the session, never saved (3: save domain
    identity).
    Edge = out port -> in port, same type.
    API: node add/remove, port create/remove/rename, connect/disconnect,
    and the list queries app_data uses today (count/at/by_key,
    connection_at, connected) - shaped like the JackPortInfo accessors so
    root_ops changes little. A generation counter for "graph changed".

G3. Cycles: connect refuses an edge that closes a loop
    (DATA_ACTION_ERR_INVALID; the peers list can leave such ports out).
    A deliberate feedback edge (1 cycle late) can come later.

G4. Process plan. After any graph change [main-thread] builds an
    immutable plan: nodes in topological order (Kahn), per input port its
    source buffers. Handed over by atomic pointer swap; [audio-thread]
    stores the plan generation it runs at cycle start (ack), [main-thread]
    frees the old plan - and buffers of removed ports - only once the ack
    passed it. The old plan can still sum a removed node's output, so
    stopping the owner is not enough.

G5. Buffers and summing, done by the plan before each node runs.
    Audio: each output port owns a max_buffer_size buffer. Input with no
    source -> shared silence, one source -> that buffer (zero copy,
    read-only to the consumer - LV2 connects it directly, CLAP copies),
    2+ -> summed into the input's own buffer.
    MIDI: one source -> that MIDI_BUF, 2+ -> midi_buf_merge.
    A node that did not write its outputs this cycle (stopped, sleeping,
    error) gets them zeroed / emptied by the plan - else downstream sums
    stale data every cycle (covers the CLAP sleeping branch too).
    Buffers are sized by the max buffer size - G8 reallocates.

G6. MIDI ports on the graph: the context-owned MIDI_BUFs from 2M move
    into the graph ports. Contexts get them through a typed accessor
    (graph_port_midi_rt) - audio and MIDI never share one void* getter.

G7. JACK at the edges = the backend. audio_backend.h, implemented by
    jack_funcs.c, picked at compile time: start the process callback and
    thread, sample rate, buffer size + their change callbacks (G8),
    exposed ports (buffers, MIDI through app_jack_midi_in_rt/out_rt), the
    foreign ports an exposed port can link to (optional - a backend
    without other programs returns none), latency of the exposed ports
    (2L L1e). Transport stays in jack_funcs until 2T.
    Exposed ports are graph endpoint nodes the backend registers: "in"
    (audio pointers / MIDI into MIDI_BUFs at cycle start) and "out"
    (summed graph inputs written to the backend buffers at the end).
    master becomes a node summing its inputs into master_out; the memcpy
    goes. trk_audio_process_rt: read messages (as now) -> run the plan.
    Contexts get the graph handle at init instead of trk_jack and stop
    including jack_funcs.h - the synth metronome and LV2 transport calls
    (app_jack_return_transport_rt) are the last users until 2T.

G8. Sample rate / buffer size changes. Today sample_rate_change only
    stores the value (its own TODO: not [thread-safe]) and there is no
    buffer size callback, so plugins are never re-activated: their
    latency (often depends on both) goes stale, and so do the CLAP min/max
    buffer sizes given at clap_plug_init. The backend's change callbacks
    hand the new values to [main-thread] via atomics; the graph
    reallocates its buffers (new plan), every activated CLAP plugin
    restarts with them, LV2 gets the rate at instantiate - a rate change
    means re-instantiating, state carried over. Sampler/synth take the
    new rate. 2L's latency is re-read through those restarts.

G9. Routing UI. root_ops CONNECT lists graph ports, plus for an exposed
    port the foreign JACK ports it can link to. action_do: two graph ports
    -> graph connect/disconnect; exposed <-> foreign -> JACK as now.
    group_key/group_label stay owner based, DATA_NS_JACK_CLIENT stays for
    foreign ports. "Ports changed" event from the graph generation
    counter, the same path app_jack_ports_sync feeds today.

G10. Later, opt-in: a small pool of aux JACK outs/ins or a per node
     "direct out" - more endpoint nodes. An external insert (aux out ->
     other program -> aux in) costs a 1 cycle round trip - report it.

    MIGRATION, each step runnable: G1 -> G2-G5 (unused until the next
    step) -> contexts create graph ports (rename the app_jack_* port calls
    at the call sites, DSP code untouched) + G6 + G7 -> G8 -> G9 -> remove
